{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Give me children</b>. The commentators (Rashi and Ibn Ezra) said that she meant: pray for me. \"And if not, I am dead\" — this is Rashi's language (Rashi on Genesis 30:1), that one who has no children is considered as dead, and it is from the Midrash of our Sages (Bereshit Rabbah 71:6). But I am astonished: if so, why was his anger kindled, and why did he say, \"Am I in place of Elohim?\" — for YHWH hears the righteous, and what Abraham our father said — he had no children but I do have children; from you He has withheld, but from me He has not withheld. Are the righteous not wont to pray on behalf of others? Behold, Elijah and Elisha prayed for foreign women (1 Kings 17:21; 2 Kings 4:33). And it appears that because of this our Sages took him to task, as they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 71:7): The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Thus do they answer those who press — by your life, your children are destined to stand before her son. According to the plain sense, Rachel said to Jacob that he should give her children, and indeed her intent was that he should pray for her — but that he should pray for her until he gives her children by all means, and if not, she would put herself to death in grief. She spoke improperly out of her passion, and she thought that because of his love for her Jacob would afflict himself, don sackcloth and ashes, and pray until she would have children, so that she would not die in her sorrow."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ושומע אל צדיקים ה׳' appears to be Ramban's parenthetical theological assertion rather than a direct quotation; translated as declarative. The reference 'אבא' (my father) likely refers to Abraham, inferred from context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:36:42.877089064Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}